# Array front insertion growth performance

Measures 1,000,000 unshift calls that each insert a value before the first
element of an array that keeps growing. The array is never drained, so the
workload measures the amortized cost of growing one contiguous allocation from
the front: an implementation that moved every remaining element on each
insertion would be quadratic here, while this representation stays linear. The
first and last elements are printed to prove the stored order.

>>> checkpoint array-benchmark-introduction 73b9eaca434ae8064ec528bf039df87bf5d57dfd
Starting point: this commit introduced the benchmark runner and the first
array workloads, giving later array changes a historical comparison.

>>> source
let values: int[] = []
for (iteration in 0..1000000) {
    values->unshift(iteration)
}
print(values[0])
print(values[999999])
